Z||000000||Richard Parnell||04-16-2000||12:47 AM||||Got the dewstrips on.  But could NOT get the two hard to remove screws back in or even started.  It looks like the rubber will stay in place ok without those screws.  If I have to I'll rubber seal along there to hold it.  Wasn't there a thread about opening up the door frame and removing a rubber stopper at the bottom of the window mechanism that would allow the window to roll all the way down?||||reg||11
Z||000001||Gokart||04-16-2000||06:30 AM||||Rich, your going to want to get back in there and get those screws in. If you don't water will eventually get down in there rusting away the metal inside the door frame. I went to the hardware store and replaced the screws with little bolts with metric heads for easier removal the next time. Rodney's tool will be great for getting the old screws out, then all you need is a small wrench to get your new metric bolts in. I used one of those "antenna" magnets to get them started. Put one on the top of the magnet and carefully got it into the hole then reached in with the wrench and got them in. Good luck. It's a pain, but regroup and do it!||||reg||4
Z||000002||^-sHarK-^||04-16-2000||10:08 AM||||Richard,  <P>I think the link your are looking for is:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001335.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001335.html</A> <P>Charles||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||04-17-2000||10:22 AM||||I dont know if you have the problem solved or not yet, but I placed those screws in with a pair of bent needle nose pliers.  Once I had them in real close to the hole, I slipped the removal tool in to press the screw in the rest of the way (angle it from the left side like 10:00 clock positon), let go of it with the pliers, and then you should be able to turn the screw in and get it started.  Once its started to can get a better bite on it.<P>I know thats kind of a messed up procedure (at least the description is) but I hope it helps.<P>I did the dew wipes on my brothers car last month, and I am planning to do mine next weekend.  Maybe Ill snap some pictures of the ordeal.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||04-18-2000||11:17 PM||||I changed mine about a month ago and, although it IS an ordeal, it is well worth it. I did the same thing, holding the screw with needlenose pliers and it worked well (that and the use of Rodney's dew strip tool) -- there are a few pictures on my site <A HREF="http://www.tgowens/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tgowens/fiero</A> ||||reg||10
